	 ('2e5c542c-080e-43d4-95c6-b8000234abe0'::uuid,'He and his love met with her and others. She noted that he had not let go of his love''s hand since their wedding. He was assigned to be on a sharpshooters squad with her and others. He suggested that her ally ask what was "Fact or Fiction" when he couldn''t tell himself. He helped the group retreat for cover and told them to leave a corpse since it would only get in the way. He also helped calm her ally down and voted not to kill him.

While on the mission, the group was attacked by reptile mutts. After she climbed out, she heard someone yell and saw him fighting with the mutts. However, he was killed before she could rescue him. She triggered a device to self-destruct. As he died, she relived brief moments of his life, including seeing a Gold parachute, a trident, his mentor''s laugh, his life on the seas of his district, and his love in her wedding dress.

After his death, his wife gave birth to their son.

Since he was a Career Tribute, it is likely he killed several tributes during his first Games and it is presumed that he killed the other Careers. However, it is not confirmed whether he joined their alliance. During the 75th Hunger Trials, he killed two other tributes.

Forced and in order to stay popular, he put on a flirtatious personality, fooling many into believing that they held his affections. If he did not oblige, someone beloved to him would be killed; however, if he did, he would be rewarded greatly. His conquests were legendary and he was famed for being able to go through four or five people in each annual visit during the Hunger Trials. However, his only true love was his fellow victor. During his last interview, he recited a poem addressed to his one true love; while many members of the audience believed they were the subject, it is implied that the poem was actually written about her. He also cared a lot for his mentor, who had volunteered to participate in the Hunger Trials, instead of his love. He was close friends with another victor and fairly good allies with others. He became a closer friend to another victor later. He married and the pair had one son together who was born after his death.

He prided himself on his ability to make almost anyone desire him, and it was this effortless charm that has made him a favorite among Capitol citizens. He appeared to always be relaxed, smiling, and an open book. He could make conversation with almost anyone. He was easy to please and entertain, and easily distracted. He himself would admit that he was flighty and shallow, although anyone who took the time to know him would discover that there he had more depth than he let on. While he got along fine with most people, when he cared about someone, he cared deeply. He was willing to put them ahead of himself and would risk his own well-being to protect his loved ones.

He was known for his frequent and varied sexual partners. When he came to the Capitol, he sometimes went through four or five lovers in one visit. For the most part, people assumed he did this for gifts or attention; however, he was forced to spend time with wealthier citizens and threatened to torture his loved ones otherwise. As such, he complied, but remained emotionally unattached to most of his trysts. His true love was his fellow victor, and he would remain monogamous to her under more ideal circumstances.','{"source": "Finnick Odair.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 3, "position": 3, "chunk_index": 3, "total_chunks": 4, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Finnick Odair.txt", "parent_document_id": "c948dc16-091c-4f46-bf41-ba092a3734a1"}','[0.043901462,0.020391429,0.024156222,0.009487217,0.01975527,-0.037965525,-0.0014010473,-0.004676429,-0.007918217,0.004459302,-0.036841407,-0.04488525,0.02625878,-0.01536726,-0.07983031,-0.019551273,0.088664874,0.03392875,0.039358128,0.052835796,-0.039757702,0.026688296,0.037232015,-0.08851444,-0.002978875,-0.0026010445,0.014258891,0.011416821,-0.13567513,-0.0035201474,0.004775719,0.02600539,-0.027332654,0.02658393,0.010022773,0.044598278,0.008476838,-0.017942421,-0.0013616431,-0.020440444,0.017233415,0.020605158,0.008295347,0.016965028,0.025989333,0.001198638,0.009943213,-0.006911217,-0.02663936,-0.01481745,0.012660395,-0.026209904,0.01782973,0.013697804,0.05178154,-0.015134317,0.020081684,0.046888713,-0.045713518,0.009699193,-0.029055526,0.029235113,0.024557857,0.013019013,-0.003312157,-0.038409803,-0.037963178,-0.029753456,0.006769213,0.021304315,0.043838996,0.00011946414,0.005589368,-0.009445291,-0.004052139,-0.078426234,-0.063266166,-0.04843625,0.016122222,0.01531577,0.011291365,-0.013825803,-0.061311316,-0.033750594,0.0048281606,-0.04222281,-0.029131215,-0.0014159511,-0.011086235,0.029080272,-0.029682528,0.025865482,0.002079869,0.030107724,0.036082424,0.0316492,0.02