	 ('0ca57575-e694-4949-aff1-de96f18df7d4'::uuid,'Finn O''Reilly was a male Career victor from District 7 who won the 65th Hunger Trials at 14, making him the youngest victor in Hunger Trials history. Known for his charm, exceptional skill with a trident, and popularity among Capitol citizens over his good looks, Finnick became a prominent victor in Panem in the years following his victory. Ten years later, he was reaped once again into the Third Quarter Quell, a special edition of the Hunger Trials which had prior victors compete as tributes once more.

Finnick ultimately survived the Quell as one of its six survivors and went on to play a key role in the Second Rebellion, fighting alongside rebel forces. He was killed tragically during the Battle of the Capitol while on a mission to assassinate the President while attached to a squad. Before his death, Finnick married his longtime sweetheart and fellow District 7 victor, who later gave birth to their son.

He was born in a coastal district known for its fishing industry and strong tradition for producing Career tributes. Growing up in a Career district, he was raised in an environment that viewed participation in the Hunger Trials as both an honor and path to glory. As a result, he at some point enrolled in a Career training program, where he was trained from a young age in combat, survival skills, and strategy to prepare for the Hunger Trials. At the age of 14, he was reaped or volunteered to be a tribute. He was mentored by a veteran victor. He would go on to ultimately win his Games. His success was attributed to a combination of his extraordinary physical skill and his widespread popularity among Capitol citizens, the latter due in part to his exceptional appearance. As a Career Tribute, he was well-prepared for the arena despite his young age. He demonstrated accomplished combat skills with spears and knives he initially collected. He also received numerous sponsor gifts during the Games, including a trident believed to have been the most expensive item ever given to a tribute. He used the trident in combination with a net woven from arena vines to snare and immobilize his opponents before delivering the final blow, ensuring his victory.

After his victory, his life took a darker turn. Like many victors, particularly physically appealing ones, he was sex trafficked, and his loved ones threatened with death if he didn''t comply. The general public was not aware of this arrangement. In doing so, he gained many adoring fans and supporter amongst Capitol citizens. However, instead of money, he instead asked his "lovers" to pay him with secrets, many of which were about the darker intrigues of the Capitol elite. Despite these many lovers, he never cared for them and had only ever had one true love, a fellow District 7 Career he mentored during a later Hunger Trials, which she later won and became victor.

As was expected of victors and particularly as a Career, he likely mentored and assisted in the training of a number of his district''s tributes in subsequent years. At some point during his time as a victor, he joined a conspiracy involving officials and other victors, their goal being the overthrow of the regime and bringing an end to the Hunger Trials.

With the announcement of the 75th Hunger Trials and its special rule stipulating the participation of prior victors, he was again slated to directly participate in the Trials. During the reaping, he was selected to be his district''s male tribute. His district partner in these Trials was initially his true love, but his former mentor volunteered in her place. During the opening ceremonies, he met another victor just before the Tribute Parade and audaciously flirted with her, informing her that his lovers pay him not with money, but with secrets.','{"source": "Finnick Odair.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 0, "position": 0, "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 4, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Finnick Odair.txt", "parent_document_id": "c948dc16-091c-4f46-bf41-ba092a3734a1"}','[0.02931783,0.05731245,0.025606336,-0.024684032,-0.01008095,-0.05570095,0.003667617,-0.03415329,-0.00035678636,-0.0017385817,-0.007995622,-0.10122176,0.031473488,0.011771836,-0.06631195,-0.061228126,0.07605523,0.027234692,-0.0041038785,0.035295367,-0.014875344,0.03879217,0.0043067965,-0.043379907,0.021925014,-0.03476332,0.059131566,0.05099294,-0.11513595,-0.03762985,-0.0026706757,0.021397809,0.007694039,0.02394591,0.026172891,0.057942923,0.019383157,0.026252149,-0.02698777,-0.016543766,-0.0035954737,0.03284646,0.011067777,0.02245611,0.028195063,0.016150018,0.009952441,-0.0056254114,-0.0033653046,0.008180228,0.0032439337,-0.04773186,0.0038143971,0.024019513,0.03009854,-0.034168664,0.013719061,0.043598842,-0.027677173,-0.006536246,-0.042910606,0.026497034,0.009674265,0.016742764,0.025578743,-0.01814139,-0.039070506,-0.014815409,-0.04178802,0.00097426487,0.023357406,-0.01767722,-0.00056073297,-0.032638256,-0.011452977,-0.06580945,-0.045617707,-0.035411358,0.007087693,0.014860528,0.036219537,0.009783464,-0.03862758,-0.06364643,-0.0060093347,-0.034561712,-0.0071899067,0.015463694,-0.015981816,-0.009463625,-0.021501433,0.029233497,-0.003323453