1959,-0.017599044,0.015709136,0.0277813]'::public.vector),
	 ('f84c9a34-9c0a-4526-ac07-7fab949cd8f0'::uuid,'Capitol commanders also began deploying their troops in positions to delay or outright block the rebel advance on the city center. This tactic became increasingly ineffective because of the rebels'' constant reinforcements. The Central City troops were under-supplied and had no replacement for soldiers they were losing. Where the Capitol troops had withdrawn, pods were expected to hold the rebel troops. This did not work, but did cause huge rebel losses. The nature of the pods was widely varied, ranging from conventional weapons such as gunfire and explosives to muttations and an unknown chemical that resembled a large black wave. Nevertheless, the rebels continued their advance.

Pressing in on the City Center
In the following weeks of battle, the Capitol''s defense began collapsing as the rebels pushed forward. During this period, the rebels marked out four distinct paths to the center of the Capitol. The Central City authorities began evacuating civilians towards the center of the city. This resulted in numerous civilian deaths as the rebels attempt to engage the Sentinel Corps escorting the evacuees. The close proximity of the civilians resulted in several bloodbaths.

City Circle bombing
After weeks of unsuccessful fighting, the remaining troops of the Capitol were ordered to retreat to the President''s Mansion and provide security for the evacuation of civilians.
As a last-ditch effort to slow down the rebel advance, the President ordered that children were to be used as a shield for his mansion. However, a hovercraft appeared and dropped what appeared to be aid parcels on the children. In reality, the aid parcels were twice-detonating explosives, which killed large numbers of children, Sentinel Corps and medics who rushed in to help. Following this, all remaining support for the President and his regime collapsed, and the Capitol was quickly overrun by the rebels.

Aftermath
The government was dissolved, as were the Armed Forces and all its institutions. Mass arrests of Sentinel Corps, officials, and designers soon followed, including the President himself, who was sentenced to be publicly executed. In what was supposed to be the liberalization and restructuration of the government, was actually a consolidation of power by a self-declared interim President. Showing authoritarian tendencies, the interim President refused to hold elections, and proposed a continuation of a punitive games system, which was narrowly approved. In a private meeting, the captured President revealed that it was, in fact, the interim President who had ordered the bombing of the children. During the execution ceremony, a last-minute decision was made to assassinate the interim President instead, while the captured President was killed by the crowd. With both leaders dead, an emergency presidential election was held. Under the new administration, a constitutional republic was formed, and the punitive games were abolished permanently. Massive amounts of economic and political liberalization took place. It was later proven that the hovercraft actually belonged to the rebels, evidenced by the lack of Capitol aircraft at the time and the strategy behind the bombing.','{"source": "Battle of the Capitol.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 1, "position": 1, "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 2, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Battle of the Capitol.txt", "parent_document_id": "f144f166-fa92-4c0d-afa8-bcf04a5e8c7e"}','[-0.03667092,0.06775398,0.0044921823,0.04475868,0.0025672223,-0.025303928,-0.009947016,0.024764389,-0.058509555,0.002393474,-0.055288818,-0.05193621,0.06009948,-0.05320534,0.031380925,0.01188595,0.02861433,0.06972903,-0.030722756,0.037492257,0.011555168,-0.030410653,-0.015550005,-0.06819146,0.018335735,0.0027918823,0.030598255,-0.05204085,-0.08618693,-0.0065458748,0.017405253,-0.012268917,-0.0038567313,