	 ('483ba849-7481-44f8-b523-aa914bf2e478'::uuid,'Another thanked for the kindness to him and his sister, said that the people were his family, and that he was not dying by choice. In an adaptation, both siblings go up at the same time. During the interview, it is stated that both turned the event into a family affair. One speaks before the other does, telling the audience that they are not going in by choice.
Another questioned the legality of the special event. In an adaptation, it is stated how much has been contributed over the years and it is not known what will be missed more. It is then said that since the event was written into law, it can be unwritten as well.
Another read a love note. According to an observer, almost all the women fainted, thinking that they were the subject. The note was indeed for a true love.
Another asked if it really is impossible to do something about the situation, seeing as when the twist was first written, it was not taken into account the bond that would inevitably form. In an adaptation, she instead expresses anger at being forced to fight again.
Another quietly pondered about how the people all think of the leader as being all-powerful and asked, if he is so powerful, why doesn''t he just change it?
Another helped spark a rebellion by stating that the leader could change it at any moment if he had wanted to.
One unexpectedly put on a show to spark a rebellion. Her wedding dress surprisingly transformed into a symbolic bird at the end of the interview. This lead to the designer''s death the next day. Prior to her interview, the dress was made fun of. In an adaptation, it was another who made fun of the dress. However, when she learns that the leader forced it to be worn, she says to make him pay.
Another had the same enjoyable back-and-forth as the year before. He talked of a secret marriage and revealed a pregnancy, both fake, but the latter being in order to get the audience''s sympathy to keep their faith.

At the close of the interviews, two victors led the others in a spontaneous show of unity by holding hands. Some, mostly the career victors, are reluctant at first, but finally the crowd into a chaotic uproar. The chain of hands, though seen to some as a symbol of the victors'' friendship and the first public show of unity since a historical period, was meant as a symbol of rebellion, saying that even the dreadfulness of the games couldn''t sever the bond the victors had formed nor destroy the united strength they held. Nonetheless, this did not stop the tributes from battling to the death once the Games began the following day.','{"source": "Interviews.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 2, "position": 2, "chunk_index": 2, "total_chunks": 3, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Interviews.txt", "parent_document_id": "a2c6f42e-fd57-44a1-a58f-f3e6f976e877"}','[-0.015122101,0.042369515,0.013775524,-0.012236442,0.013504572,-0.018982613,-0.02141944,0.015733285,-0.01732698,0.033409078,-0.039243925,-0.034028143,0.020039164,-0.056552134,-0.03885045,0.01596181,0.028047591,0.026066033,0.015656525,0.08287519,-0.027330164,0.067684434,0.022634579,-0.09318263,-0.00039201093,-0.006313047,0.0472685,-0.022342639,-0.09808646,0.054240804,0.019679783,0.009475614,-0.029246965,0.060671985,-0.013658689,0.031962223,0.0047674053,-0.026937675,0.017429078,-0.01958931,0.028931526,0.0036738375,-0.004227838,-0.0067555686,0.029242247,0.003411705,0.028971175,-0.027703488,0.013055035,-0.010536062,0.012428568,-0.04494975,-0.013455425,-0.016347818,0.03694748,0.011595114,0.021344908,-0.0038267558,-0.034373745,-0.0015436527,-0.022387212,0.005664605,0.019069597,0.0403564,0.008187996,-0.016878838,-0.013669586,-0.031809743,-0.015170798,0.016317839,0.05382735,-0.026488556,-9.941044e-05,0.016313342,0.03225169,-0.06968823,-0.07720578,-0.02535447,-0.020470597,0.022675836,0.009624638,-0.017624762,-0.039056957,-0.034899615,0.017429385,-0.05667884,0.001416132