	 ('f146f093-f537-4519-8246-88218c03907c'::uuid,'The introduction of Hybrid Mutants in the 10th Games would likewise return as another hazard tributes would have to face, enabling Gamemakers to construct elaborate battlegrounds that became as much of the spectacle as the tributes themselves.

The Games were also punctuated by 25-Year Hunger Games, special editions of the Hunger Games held every twenty-five years. These special events introduced unique twists "written into the Treaty of Dominion" that changed the regular formula of the Hunger Games while also serving to remind the districts of their transgression during the Crimson Conflict. The 25th Hunger Games required the districts to vote on the tributes they would send, while the 50th Hunger Games doubled the number of tributes (48 instead of 24), producing the bloodiest contests in the Hunger Games history.

Through these measures, the Hunger Games matured into a complex apparatus of oppression for the districts while simultaneously becoming a national pastime adored by Capitol citizens. The combination of ritual, spectacle, and technological display reinforced the image of the Capitol''s supremacy. By the time of the 74th Hunger Games, the institution had reached its most polished form—complete with pageantry, televised interviews, advanced Coliseum design, and carefully engineered Hybrid Mutants but also its most precarious, as victors and districts alike began to chafe under the very system meant to keep them subdued after so many years.

The 74th Hunger Games marked the beginning of the end for the Capitol''s long-standing institution. For the first time in history, two tributes were crowned as joint victors after threatening an attempted double suicide with poisonous nightlock berries rather than kill one another. While outwardly portrayed as an act of love for one another, a notion readily accepted by Capitol audiences, to the districts and Capitol political leaders, it was seen as an act of defiance that undermined the Capitol''s authority. The two victors were subsequently transformed into symbols of resistance, fomenting unrest in the districts not seen since the Crimson Conflict.

The spectacle of dual victors destabilized the Capitol''s narrative. The threat posed by a living symbol of defiance led to efforts to reassert control and subdue unrest. When this failed, the upcoming 75th Hunger Games, designated as the Third Quarter Quell, was utilized to eliminate the threat posed by victors as a whole. For this Quell, the Capitol decreed that the pool of tributes would be drawn exclusively from existing victors, forcing the victors back into the Coliseum.

Instead of restoring order, however, the Quell accelerated the rebellion. A covert alliance of victors coordinated an escape plan. When the Energy Barrier enclosing the Coliseum was destroyed, the Quell collapsed mid-contest without a victor. Several tributes were extracted by rebel forces, while those left behind fell into Capitol custody.

The 75th Hunger Games thus became the first and only Games to conclude without the crowning of a victor. Its abrupt end as the last formal Hunger Games held marked not only the collapse of the Games as an institution but also the beginning of the Second Rebellion.

In the endgame of the Second Rebellion, the Capitol was invaded by rebel forces and the regime was subsequently overthrown. During the formation of an interim government, the surviving victors were asked to vote on a proposal for a symbolic and final Annual Hunger Games, in which the children of prominent Capitol officials would serve as tributes. The measure was narrowly approved by the victors assembled, reflecting both the deep trauma left by the Games and a desire to perpetuate their logic of retribution while solidifying power.

While approved, the plan was never realized. During a public execution, the interim leader was assassinated. With these events, the Hunger Games as an institution definitively collapsed.

Following an emergency election, a new president was chosen. Under new leadership, the Hunger Games were formally abolished, the Treaty of Dominion repealed, and the former arenas demolished.','{"source": "Hunger Games (event).txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 2, "position": 2, "chunk_index": 2, "total_chunks": 6, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Hunger Games (event).txt", "parent_document_id": "fdc007e2-6469-4722-a070-803445e42e92"}','[0.022277966,0.088703975,-0.01945578,0.006820586,0.030518675,-0.014260996,-0.046476163,0.033134542,0.0072399033,0.028650228,-0.05437822,-0.055373445,0.024914756,-0.021751143,-0.019120738,-0.060425755,0.013236658,0.048265625,-0.046267197,0.05128116,0.01337767,0.020519115,-0.01761147,-0.03545833,0.025168438,-0.021178985,0.023575118,-0.022533968,-0.113264605,-0.007945869,0.009480836,-0.004202046,-0.0057520755,0.06594559,-0.0040631224,0.017249698,-0.014652374,-0.0046284115,0.013467697,0.00016561865,0.029868843,-0.0148823755,0.04988509,0.009506763,0.028838191,0.021311821,0.011913015,-0.0070475508,0.009697511,-0.019823268,0.004123445,-0.04380446,-0.029357495,0.028813183,0.04524844,0.01794455,7.703274e-05,0.022236722,-0.043418493,-0.007560738,-0.03502707,0.013065189,0.0053754477,0.0454379,0.013759146,-0.017905712,-0.014868691,-0.046558317,-0.009321199,0.013402352,0.0605799,-0.022205336,0.03465225,-0.0074423063,0.007841513,-0.05333434,-0.042867932,-0.031558633,-0.0016198416,0.034511972,0.028182382,-0.04130302,-0.027198428,-0.031002687,0.072358094,-0.031122549,-0.010746888,-0.009340478,0.017646272,-0.022928001,-0.022502536,0.03476767,-0.019877499,0.02926518,0.0058113486,-0.017241867,0.0071062446,0.0066333497,-0.018900182,0.024190694,-0.012937926,0.025443753,-0.011899666,0.0039191474,0.008580036,-0.01571882,0.012186758,-0.009819142,0.047836337,-0.015952818,-0.014961662,-0.036596704,0.023378713,0.018202694,-0.019989334,-0.009679823,-0.010212762,0.021981843,0.021967804,0.042800184,0.00167