ublic.vector),
	 ('303dc21d-344a-466e-8a2d-d5fa51818cd9'::uuid,'In their place, memorials were erected to honor the tributes who had perished across seventy-five years of sadistic bloodshed, marking the end of the Capitol''s most infamous instrument of control.

The Opening Ceremonies lasted one week, starting with the reaping on July 4th and ending with a night of interviews in the Capitol on July 10th.

Every year on July 4th, the entire population of each district was required to assemble at their respective Justice Building for the reaping. Only those who were deathly ill were excused, and if a citizen was found lying, they would be imprisoned. District children between the age of 12 and 18 were separated into pens according to age, under Sentinel Corps supervision, while adults were herded to surrounding areas. Two large glass bowls were placed at the front of the assembly gathering—one containing slips with the names of all eligible boys, the other with those of all eligible girls.

The number of entries in the reaping bowls was cumulative with age. A twelve-year-old child had a single slip; a thirteen-year-old, two slips, continuing until eighteen, when a child''s name appeared seven times. In addition, children could volunteer for Token—a system of ration incentives that allowed them to exchange additional entries for grain and oil sufficient to feed one family member for a year. The Token system was cumulative across years and for the number of family members it was taken out for, meaning repeated claims rapidly multiplied a child''s risk of being reaped.

The district''s Capitol escort presided over the ceremony, drawing one slip from each bowl following the mayor''s recitation of the Treaty of Dominion. The names called become that year''s tributes for the district unless another eligible child volunteered in their place. In most districts, volunteering was rare, as participation in the Games was regarded as a near-certain death sentence. Exceptions were found the so-called Career districts where children trained extensively in combat and survival with the expectation of volunteering. In these districts, the Games were framed as a path to glory, and tributes often competed for the honor of taking the stage.

Children became ineligible for the reaping once they passed their eighteenth year. Similarly, victors of previous Games were exempt from all future reapings, even if they remained within the eligible reaping age range, though many later served as mentors to new tributes.

After the reaping, tributes were escorted into the Justice Building for a brief farewell period. Each tribute was allotted one hour to say goodbye to family and friends in private rooms under Sentinel Corps guard. At the conclusion of this period, they were escorted to waiting Capitol vehicles and transported directly to the district train station, where they boarded a luxury train bound for the Capitol.

The length of the journey depended on the district''s distance from the Capitol. For remote districts, the trip lasted a full day, while tributes from districts closer to the Capitol endured much shorter rides.

The journey marked the tributes'' first exposure to Capitol excess. Each train was outfitted with extravagant dining halls, private compartments, opulent furniture, and amenities typically unavailable to district citizens. Tributes were served lavish buffets and meals—a pointed contrast to the hunger and scarcity experienced in Aurelia''s poorest districts—and given fully equipped quarters with hot baths and full wardrobes. Capitol staff were present to serve the tributes and attend to their every need or desire, further underscoring the stark divide between Capitol luxury and district deprivation.

During the ride, tributes typically met their assigned mentor, a victor from their district who would advise them throughout their journey into the Games. Mentors introduced tributes to the politics of survival in the Capitol and in the arena, including the importance of sponsors. They also brainstormed their tributes'' strategy for the Games and how to best market them.','{"source": "Hunger Games (event).txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 3, "position": 3, "chunk_index": 3, "total_chunks": 6, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Hunger Games (event).txt", "parent_document_id": "fdc007e2-6469-4722-a070-803445e42e92"}','[0.08612253,0.103755236,0.026398754,0.011955722,-0.044483136,0.016285527,0.0019130786,-0.0020768086,0.02282851,0.04754341,-0.042549986,-0.05164856,0.043893635,-0.05547675,-0.021908438,-0.05831765,-0.018955419,0.02644699,-0.027364075,0.048192587,0.006117291,0.049527433,0.025899164,-0.046227347,0.06103642,-0.019470967,0.075518385,0.0057102884,-0.072398946,0.0035708048,-0.029687617,-0.034397077,-0.015320682,0.05696385,-0.008202203,0.0013923664,-0.01312989,-0.008033444,0.00090672425,-0.01101659,0.00019672522,0.022672307,-0.02456117,0.020808013,-0.016092336,0.038808893,0.005066152,-0.0014296708,-0.03387345,0.001585448,0.008664861,-0.048625275,-0.039617285,0.024674457,0.027448637,0.00682919,-0.0041459785,0.039899632,-0.026397107,-0.00014551364,-0.024237826,0.003607889,-0.023522776,0.034484092,-0.009612003,-0.0058607664,-0.018371725,-0.02723084,-0.031647205,-0.004496478,0.019132951,0.00012788021,0.03735674,0.019318763,0.03289899,-0.019498246,-0.052716717,-0.04943009,0.00232668,0.03299975,-0.0002659862,-0.00833211,-0.009394878,-0.027041955,0.015233922,-0.021015028,0.003414809,-0.013978248,-0.034537468,-0.0014971884,-0.010615497,-0.008863317,-0.022485035,0.05650284,0.029575326,0.03290998,-0.008089234,0.0024592106,-0.03817694,0.006636458,-0.026420744,0.024401529,0.0034586925,-0.010477199,0.020395938,0.000585