	 ('13afa43e-5df2-450a-8e5a-698679f34352'::uuid,'District 7 was the smallest and poorest of the thirteen districts of Aurelia. Its main industry is coal mining, but after the Second Rebellion, their main industry was converted to medicine. It had a population of around 8,000 people prior to being firebombed.

Since District 7 was the poorest of Aurelia''s districts, its tributes were almost guaranteed a quick death. Prior to the 74th Hunger Games, the district had produced only two victors. Most of its tributes were malnourished, impoverished, and unaccustomed to glamour. Most District 7 tributes were inexperienced with weapons compared to other districts, holding them at a major disadvantage since their residents were only allowed to become miners at 18. Sponsors typically refused to support District 7 tributes given how rarely they had stood out or succeeded in the past.

Following the events of the third 25-Year Hunger Games, the The Central City bombed District 7, only sparing the Champion''s Village. Escaping citizens were able to tear down the fence and evacuate about 800 people; however, the majority of the district''s people were killed in the firebombing. After its destruction, the rebels utilized District 7 as a transportation base to send troops to the The Central City.

After Aurelia''s second war, people returned to District 7. A mass grave was dug for victims of the bombing, and a medicine factory was constructed as District 7 began to rebuild.

The Gutter was the poorest part of District 7. Food was extremely difficult to acquire and almost all families lived in abject poverty. Many who lived there lost families due to mining accidents or from starvation. They only had electricity a few hours a day, so they mostly used candles for light. As a result, any adult who reached elderhood was venerated. In contrast, those who were well fed and young were scorned. Early in the mornings, the streets of the Seam were crowded with exhausted coal miners heading to work, who faced the lethal risks of collapsing caverns and noxious fumes on a daily basis. One safeguard in the mines was a canary who would sing - when the canary went silent, it indicated the mine air was no longer safe to breathe. With the primary outputs of coal in the region, everything in the Seam seemed to be coated in a layer of coal dust.

The square was where the district''s merchant class lived, above their places of work. All the district''s citizens would be forced to gather in the square to watch that year''s annual reaping, which took place on the stage out front of the Justice Building. The Justice Building was used for formal administrative tasks, including marriage registrations. Tributes had an hour to say goodbye to their loved ones inside, and the eldest children of miners killed in accidents received medals of honor here.

The Den was the district''s black market. It was burned down by Sentinel Corps.

The Champion''s Village is a small area where the victors of previous Hunger Trials dwell. The houses are grand and luxurious compared to the rest of District 7; however, the village was wholly neglected for years due to a lack of victors.

The sole settlement was surrounded by a twenty-foot high electric fence, though it was rarely on due to power shortages. While officials inform the public that the fence was meant to ward away wild animals, it was more likely to prevent people from escaping out into the wilderness. When a new Head Commander arrived, the fence''s electricity was reactivated. It was later torn down by escaping refugees.

This scruffy field just inside the fence sits near a house in the Seam. A number of plants and flowers grow there, both edible and medicinal. This was a main entry point into the woods. After the war, it became a mass grave for those who died in the bombing, but the plants there regrew.','{"source": "District 12.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 0, "position": 0, "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 2, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/District 12.txt", "parent_document_id": "c0532055-699f-444c-bc68-10cb4afef7c5"}','[-0.010094707,0.12326571,0.001402449,-0.0023189418,-0.01923947,-0.027398236,0.0040256595,0.027596563,-0.012955336,0.024912214,-0.013504121,-0.06443858,0.024415467,-0.028147824,0.008259548,-0.020766333,0.02426949,0.021243745,-0.019858588,0.10065387,0.067345224,-0.0057229954,0.013306034,-0.066699095,-0.02203712,-0.013048913,0.025006512,0.013931776,-0.07111828,-0.026889453,-0.003481428,-0.045895074,0.016698482,0.046562787,-0.0021762475,0.0067920117,-0.020869322,0.008222725,0.0067419736,-0.008299955,0.0070505887,0.0002963468,0.01678551,-0.0070268204,0.0010167023,-0.0016660665,-0.004793437,-0.026633063,-0.004336424,-0.008448204,0.0012495902,-0.05327314,-0.008302479,0.020527894,0.04749723,0.0046013542,0.020783436,0.03177739,-0.014059849,-0.004142751,-0.00687681,0.01813553,0.02058963,0.014034371,0.033178236,-0.03142922,0.019288296,-0.044400398,-0.018629557,-0.03036729,0.0229966,0.00892656,-0.005149754,0.0059445933,0.0348932,-0.015512249,-0.05130096,-0.05200984,0.015252246,-0.007049873,0.023196831,-0.035749756,-0.061341353,-0.0037150246,0.0026373155,-0.0386679,0.0026889122,-0.0043177456,-0.0284345,-0.023539966,-0.008287116,0.0028235759,-0.025395082,0.045719564,0.01247912,-0.0076408708,0.035430536,0.005687045,-0.0285033,0.025957504,-0.024011228,0.033432335,-0.05624001,-0.002944083,0.01966779,-0.022687357,-0.029812219,-0.019643033,0.0376489,-0.07622961,-0.017905364,0.004087717,0.0009791246,-0.012752365,-0.0333