	 ('71f1de60-0f2f-431e-ad4c-fa07077ef1fd'::uuid,'Other notable locations included the The execution oak, the school, the graveyard, and entrances to the mines.

The Triple-finger salute is a symbol made with the three middle fingers of your left hand, bringing them to your lips, and then holding them out to another person. In District 7, it''s an expression of admiration, gratitude, and a way to say goodbye to someone you love.

Due to poverty, most people don''t own books, so they pass on their stories through songs. By a certain time, songs are largely discouraged.

Weddings were simple events conducted with humble materials. Traditionally, women would rent a well-worn white dress, while men donned clothing outside of their normal mining clothes. The couple would register as spouses at the Justice Building to be assigned a home. For a ceremony, there would be a small gathering of close friends and family members enjoying a meal and if available, a piece of cake. The reception would then sing a traditional song to welcome the couple into their new home as they crossed the threshold. Afterwards, the newlyweds would make their first fire and do the traditional toasting of bread to share together.

The Harvest Festival is always celebrated on the last day of the Triumph Tour, typically observed with a meal at home or with a few friends if one can afford it. As decoration, people affix bunches of brightly colored corn to their front doors. For the conclusion of the tour, Harvest Festival celebrations were a public affair put on by the The Central City.

A bell was always rung in the Justice Building on New Year''s Day. It was also seen as an occasion special enough to warrant a cake from the bakery, if one could afford it.

At the time of the prequel, a nomadic minority group had been forced to settle in District 7. They had a number of their own unique cultural practices, such as their names, colorful clothes, and musical performances. Some of their songs still survived at the time of the trilogy. District 7''s first victor was a member of this group.

