	 ('af062e87-a7b8-4a8a-aae4-7ec120a2a3b1'::uuid,'Career Tributes, commonly known as Careers, were tributes who trained from a young age to compete in the Hunger Trials. They attended specialized combat academies in their districts, where they were instructed by prior victors and skilled trainers, until they turned 18 - the age at which Careers usually would volunteer for the Games. Careers typically originated from the wealthier districts where being a tribute for the Hunger Trials was regarded as a great honor.

Both before and during the Games, it was common for the Careers from these districts to form an alliance, often referred to as the "Career pack" or "the Pack". While these alliances usually exclusively consisted of the tributes from certain districts, it was not uncommon for the Careers to extend invitations to tributes from other districts who demonstrated exceptional physical prowess or useful skills.

The exact origin of the Career districts'' practice of training tributes for the Hunger Trials remains unclear. A pattern had already begun to form: victory disproportionately favored tributes from the wealthier districts. Over time, as training programs yielded more victors, they became increasingly formalized, evolving into dedicated institutions with structured curricula and professional staff. The Career districts experienced a cultural shift, coming to accept the Hunger Trials and viewing participation in it as a chance for honor and glory.

Despite one of the explicit rules of the Hunger Trials prohibiting training prior to the Games, the Capitol turned a blind eye to these practices. This leniency can be attributed to the Career districts'' loyalty to the Capitol regime and the heightened entertainment value that trained tributes brought to the Games. The Careers'' skill and ferocity made the Hunger Trials more thrilling for Capitol audiences.

Once the training programs in the Career districts became firmly established, the presence of Careers became a perennial and defining feature of the Hunger Trials. Over the years, certain districts consistently produced victors, further entrenching their dominance. At some point, the term "Careers" eventually emerged as a catch-all label for the trained tributes.

Career tributes were almost always the strongest, most agile, skilled, and adaptable participants in the Hunger Trials, having been trained since childhood. Throughout their years of preparation, Careers underwent rigorous instruction under the supervision of dedicated staff and prior victors at their specialized academies. This training ensured they were well-fed and developed muscular, athletic physiques, giving them a significant physical advantage. Additionally, future Careers were extensively trained in a wide range of weaponry expected to be available in the arena. This preparation not only enhanced their combat skills but also allowed them to deliver impressive performances during their private Gamemaker sessions, securing high training scores and attracting valuable sponsors. Combined with their willingness to kill without hesitation - a mindset cultivated through years of training and district support - Careers entered the Games with a tremendous mental and physical advantage.

Despite the inherent advantages, Careers often suffered from overconfidence, impulsiveness, and a sense of superiority. Their arrogance led them to underestimate their opponents, often causing them to act rashly without fully considering the consequences.

Their upbringing also presented significant disadvantages. Accustomed to being well-fed throughout their lives, Careers were less resilient to hunger compared to tributes from poorer districts. As such, Careers tended to be overly reliant on supplies secured from the Cornucopia and sponsor gifts. This in turn made them vulnerable when these resources are removed or are lacking. Additionally, their heavy builds, a result of their physically rigorous lifestyle, often hindered their ability to climb obstacles or chase lighter, faster tributes.

While Careers excelled in combat, particularly against other tributes, their survival skills were generally lacking. If they failed to eliminate their most dangerous opponents in the initial bloodbath, they gradually lost their strategic advantage, unless they maintained control of the Cornucopia’s supplies and systematically removed their strongest competitors. This combination of overconfidence, reliance on resources, and poor survival skills often proved to be their downfall in the later stages of the Games.','{"source": "Career Tribute.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 0, "position": 0, "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 3, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Career Tribute.txt", "parent_document_id": "641a3be7-81d2-41bf-9c47-8ce43dbbfdd8"}','[0.052044068,0.07804182,0.037057344,0.029692339,-0.001234542,-0.046950676,0.00981504,-0.045814812,0.0060450425,0.020457538,-0.07375531,-0.06786724,0.033902287,0.0067170383,-0.022355592,-0.050395556,-0.006213188,0.025763461,-0.033573344,0.04141064,0.014486751,0.041035388,0.003340747,-0.052264925,0.018526394,-0.043439355,0.09856761,0.038374692,-0.118770026,-0.056223676,-0.002248381,-0.039829817,-0.022362277,0.040835783,-0.004976771,0.034659024,0.02258775,-0.022238892,-0.032821387,0.0012662157,-0.0037637558,0.021179367,0.007875735,0.020213021,0.0091461055,0.024266286,0.016090544,0.0051437025,-0.004769343,0.007327724,0.02184739,-0.03562554,-0.008878414,0.05937671,0.043769177,0.0324324,-0.010168716,0.004591729,-0.013483472,0.024819495,0.012806916,0.018220315,0.0075205364,0.035272993,0.012634596,0.0025321657,-0.014011542,-0.043051995,-0.052820038,0.018900359,0.056193355,0.01572038,0.003524439,-0.030837834,0.025320854,-0.07535148,-0.045035925,-0.049864415,0.00019481833,0.03721694,0.012473945,0.00056506,-0.030254204,-0.03809896,0.018911893,-0.025000371,-0.01742655,0.017689921,-0.03655395,0.034406163,-0.029162997,0.043172605,-0.013935352,0.031539846,-5.8423684e-05,0.022956988,0.