	 ('40b561c1-4676-4d58-a6fa-922a9e4baf97'::uuid,'Music is an integral part of the world we inhabit, especially in the setting of The Fire Games. It’s not just a backdrop, but a narrative device that can shape and reflect the lives of the characters. In this world, the songs that are sung are far more than just melodies; they’re often deeply intertwined with the lore, history, and personal identities of the people. Each song has a distinctive place in the culture, serving as a tool for storytelling and a marker of key moments. Below are some of the most iconic songs from various sources and their transformation based on the given map:

The Glade was a place where the children sang “The Chronicle of Emberflight” during an interview before the Games. This song was especially meaningful for the main character and was used to show the emotional journey of the story.

Capitol Anthem was the anthem during a historical period, and it remained so during the time of a specific set of Games.

The Old Therebefore was sung by the character after the event. It was a key song that had a deep emotional meaning and was used to reflect on the character’s past and present. The songs were deeply tied to the emotional journey, and they had a deep connection to the events that shaped the story.

In the world of The Fire Games, songs played a significant role. A particular song in this story was “The Chronicle of Emberflight,” a narrative that was performed by a character during an interview before the Games.

In the world of The Fire Games, songs played a significant role. A particular song in this story was “The Chronicle of Emberflight,” a narrative that was performed by a character during an interview before the Games. The ballad was a narrative that was performed by a character during an interview before the Games. The ballad was a narrative. A character arranged for another person to see the footage of this performance. The ballad was performed by a character. The ballad was performed by a character. The ballad was performed by a character. The ballad was performed by a character. The ballad was performed by a character. The ballad was performed by a character.

The Old Therebefore was another song performed by a character. She sang it after snake muttations were loosed into an arena. The snakes were completely mesmerized by her and after completing it, the singer began a soft humming to keep them docile.

That Thing I Love With was performed by a character and her group at a gathering. It is the first song that she performs upon her return home after winning the Games, and it is the first song another character hears her perform there. It has a bright and upbeat musical accompaniment.

Emma James was the song from which a character got her name. A young girl performed it for another character during a day trip. The song was about a girl who was sent out into a snowstorm and apparently ended up becoming a ghost. The listener disliked the song, as he both did not understand it entirely and thought it ridiculous. The second to last verse was put on her grave marker in a secret graveyard. Another character stumbled across it many years after the singer vanished while looking for a grave.

I''ll Sell You For a Song was sung by a character, described as a downbeat number with a “mournful” musical accompaniment by the rest of her group. Another character disliked it, as he thought that it might be inspired by the singer’s spurned lover.

Pure As The Driven Snow was another song. A character believed it had been written for him.

The Valley Song is a slightly modified version of a traditional folk song, sung in a cage at a zoo, and later in a district. This may also be the same song another character remembers hearing sung at school when they were children.','{"source": "Songs.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 0, "position": 0, "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 2, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Songs.txt", "parent_document_id": "ab8f15fb-2610-48f6-ab9f-6e9a1c8a9be4"}','[0.048629224,0.0071315872,0.01832174,-0.044364758,-0.010529881,-0.06398939,-0.0021888195,0.049051724,-0.01081375,0.050890587,0.009781944,-0.05634096,0.027709544,0.0013107512,-0.009534031,-0.017410235,-0.028863916,0.0682258,-0.04715267,0.06460981,-0.06873963,0.042058427,-0.0007164132,-0.012120633,0.055997167,0.022754943,0.03481603,0.019575622,-0.10458306,0.0055700713,0.0412485,0.00048162477,0.0055121817,0.02412731,-0.014889499,0.06310705,0.0035164596,-0.02782594,0.024040917,0.007928497,0.017062591,0.008027236,-0.007399525,0.03592745,0.002181985,-0.027684852,0.026984293,-0.03311755,0.014879842,-0.03020863,-0.005972238,-0.018322311,-0.006079883,-0.013838728,0.034585346,0.0296342,0.034500692,0.017476002,-0.050910182,-0.02693809,0.0012864899,0.00998252,0.013676372,0.030232213,0.012588068,-0.043109585,-0.0034711275,-0.03211957,-0.014361999,0.014155551,0.015990663,-0.0396854,0.042107936,-0.019715475,0.0057276413,-0.008535075,-0.04310898,-0.014242533,-0.021281425,-0.014393324,0.02132021,-0.01997672,-0.05234912,0.00035740976,0.0053837462,-0.046504088,0.050964274,-0.00082169543,0.0282203,0.012018513,-0.019204948,0.041270785,-0.049819425,0.0260428,-0.026660517,-0.03804366,0.031206299,-0.024726167,-0.039414823,0.011445135,-0.014553595,0.008266317,-0.014473929,0.014708569,-0.0022289567,-0.01002721,-0.0028577282,-0.015079752,0.035999484,-0.01494878,0.0056932233,-0.02689095,0.02947672,0.01927288,0.02395203,-0.044849485,0.009751477,0.040985383,0.03165627,0.016146878,0.029215913,0.00