	 ('08ad8510-8ada-4800-8beb-82c2bca18b7f'::uuid,'Such a right was reserved for the political elites, as well as for participants and their mentors during the event and related tours. The police force could be deployed anywhere in the nation for their duties.
The capital citizens were also prohibited from traveling into the districts.

Weapon ownership is largely prohibited, with the exception of utilitarian weapons such as knives, or tools necessary for certain industries.
The penalty for illegal weapons possession was death.
Despite such a strict prohibition, several citizens of the districts are known to be illegally hoarding weapons.

Citizens who were accused of crimes were often immediately captured and publicly executed for their acts, with no evidence that they were tried by jury. Extrajudicial executions at the hands of security forces are extremely common for a variety of crimes.

The capital was known to use torture on political dissidents and rebels.
Capital punishment was routinely employed for a variety of offenses, and executions were carried out through a variety of means. There was a long list of crimes punishable by death, including dissent, armed rebellion, criticism of government, unauthorized travel between the districts, weapon ownership, and theft.
Much of the time, executions took place in the form of extrajudicial killings by the police force. The capital was not known to set any minimum age nor exemptions from the death penalty, as all those who violated its laws were executed.

Schools within the districts are extremely limited in curriculum, and serve as mostly indoctrination centers. Rather than educate students, they mostly serve to propagate propaganda to the young, where they are only taught the official narrative of events.
In the capital, there is known to be institutions of higher education. While capital citizens receive a higher level of education than the districts, they are still known to be fed state propaganda, and educators are required to instill loyalty to the regime in the minds of students.','{"source": "Human rights in Panem.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 1, "position": 1, "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 2, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Human rights in Panem.txt", "parent_document_id": "a8ae3b40-7eb2-45ec-a1a5-aaa8e9329701"}','[-0.038400657,0.10991767,-0.03634327,0.047208324,-0.013872788,0.029084312,-0.003100436,0.013103908,-0.06552009,-0.011522856,-0.0869989,-0.052418455,0.04380763,-0.04404531,-0.008020732,0.033077348,0.00440927,-0.01015842,-0.043105666,0.05470929,0.018142449,0.04847013,0.004424521,-0.029415127,-0.007842587,-0.0072678984,0.024162157,-0.022072418,-0.015124364,0.013572862,0.019658815,-0.039790247,-0.017946906,0.06915235,-0.0022794814,0.01605048,0.002436942,0.01058578,0.004992555,-0.0017729109,0.009409704,0.0105534615,0.01930885,0.027588764,-0.001453263,0.011221961,-0.024513798,0.004452056,-0.00024563374,-0.01232467,0.017556097,-0.04271118,0.0037542987,0.012680573,0.026126869,0.023671316,0.019802932,0.017096134,-0.01498558,-0.013692894,-0.01729916,0.013492846,-0.014189312,-0.011762648,0.003794403,-0.021406293,-0.019779406,-0.07585209,-0.019264406,-0.005514909,0.00278151,-0.0029304903,0.0035505355,0.033269487,0.01881968,-0.011715396,-0.043591846,-0.018638179,0.007104943,0.015210298,-0.002621295,-0.032371253,-0.028288992,-0.018890575,0.049968794,-0.027599843,-0.03883758,-0.0070239995,-0.022237372,0.00831049,-0.055761233,-0.0006678857,0.00997932,0.035114747,-0.019272985,0.06768782,-0.0010600373,-0.024087697,-0.015486359,0.023310965,-0.00021248354,0.021039804,0.022612235,-0.012396283,0.009899036,0.01674651,-0.03433265,0.018332317,0.03239417,0.0060716444,-0.01212439,-0.011809512,0.020137785,0.036