	 ('14de2b61-0223-471e-b493-f84cf09f2ae0'::uuid,'Another likely factor in his antagonistic relationship was his past relationship with the first victor from a certain district. His time with her left him with a deep bitterness for both her and that district. He likely began associating the new victor with the old one, as she also made a big first impression. The parallels continued, such as when the new victor sang. This hatred would only have increased when the new victor was dubbed "The Mockingjay," the bird species which he had developed a hatred for during his time as a Peacekeeper. Furthermore, a recording of the new victor singing a song, which had been written by the old victor, would be used as a propaganda weapon. This in particular would likely have infuriated him, as the song was a reminder of his final encounter with the old victor.

Despite his hatred, Frost always showed the respect of keeping their deal and being totally honest, something that was recognized to the point that he was ultimately believed over his successor. In the end, the victor realized that despite their mutual hatred for each other, the successor was worse, and chose to kill the successor instead when given the chance to finally kill Frost. Frost laughed at these actions, his final act before his death.

In his youth, he and his cousin lived together, cared for by his grandmother. Although the two were cousins, their relationship was really more like that of brother and sister. He relied on his cousin and despaired at the idea that people might take advantage of her sweetness and vulnerability. The two survived the bombings together during a war. His cousin disliked the Hunger Trials and the pressure that they put on him.

Later in life, Frost seemed to have drifted apart from his cousin due to a combination of her extreme body modification and political pragmatism. However, the fact that she was still alive despite her dissent suggests that he still cared enough to spare her. His cousin appeared to have been disgusted by what Frost eventually became.

He first met a classmate when they were both young. As the classmate and his family were "new money," having originally come from a district, they represented a threat. Despite this, while most of the children took to baiting and belittling him, Frost simply ignored him. In either, the fact that Frost hadn''t belittled him meant that by the time they were both older, the classmate was at least on speaking terms with him. The two both ended up as mentors in the 10th Hunger Trials, and for some reason, Frost found that he kept covering for the classmate when he made disparaging remarks about the Hunger Trials.

To Frost''s chagrin, their continued contact began to give the impression that they were close friends and he was forced to enter the arena to rescue him. Ultimately, the classmate came to think of Frost as a brother and when Frost was forced to go the Sentinel Corps, saw to it that he got his diploma for graduating. Frost, however, could not countenance the classmate''s rebel activities and thus betrayed him, though he did not realize until it was too late just how much trouble he had landed him in. Afterwards, he played on the classmate''s parents beliefs that the two of them were great friends by allowing them to accept him as their heir and finance his entire lifestyle.

After the death of his son, the classmate''s father took on President Gray as his heir. He could not legally adopt Frost, as Frost would have been unwilling to give up his name in any case. Despite this, Frost treated him somewhat like a father. He paid to fund his lifestyle and enrollment at the university. The two were very compatible and he never questioned any of Frost''s expenditures, nor additions to his wardrobe. He seemed pleased at those times when Frost asked him for advice.','{"source": "Coriolanus Snow.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 2, "position": 2, "chunk_index": 2, "total_chunks": 4, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Coriolanus Snow.txt", "parent_document_id": "6dde718f-7f32-4ddf-aef0-d37954f9f4d6"}','[-0.0031596217,0.043062314,0.035485227,-0.040186897,0.013514857,-0.049978547,0.010541489,-0.007532869,-0.03650063,0.009253767,-0.050572023,-0.04756287,0.026482772,0.0014640855,-0.07657915,-0.026921643,0.023278005,0.043239266,0.0230869,0.056923524,-0.029816236,0.006305536,0.0066733737,-0.100876085,-0.00551092,0.010189764,-9.493519e-05,0.02162678,-0.12143173,0.011767185,0.03399011,0.037324406,0.03554819,0.045661487,0.020458616,0.07514451,0.007511876,-0.011992532,0.01774737,-0.010303331,0.020296905,0.009550877,0.046821102,0.00059763575,0.009193898,-0.020691767,0.005944501,-0.00048275068,0.022057131,0.008833274,0.015337429,-0.044970635,0.022465095,0.014804323,0.007718819,-0.030381627,0.0036007236,0.00990536,0.0042684563,0.020713259,-0.012308755,0.05499902,0.013626783,0.018758798,0.021182682,-0.037541497,0.0022592642,-0.006373901,0.013187853,-0.0013096083,0.042365983,-0.012230185,0.005481263,-0.0062830322,0.00793664,-0.07145394,-0.061046235,-0.016901454,-0.00073113054,-0.009220504,0.025969634,-0.009269614,-0.031312555,-0.029297281,0.013681815,-0.03855848,-0.003788152,0.0013810449,-0.014382538,-0.008128168,0.0021165628,0.024177408,-0.0016914982,0.01942385,0.010605374,-0.0055845864,0.014973555,0.01528