	 ('3648b776-bb88-4247-9a9c-22ddbf1cf53b'::uuid,'He first encountered a Head Gamemaker during a class field trip when he was nine years old, and he watched in horror as she melted the flesh off a lab rat. He first met her in person shortly before the 10th Hunger Trials. She seemed impressed by his nerve, saying that he could one day be a Gamemaker. She said that he understood the importance of keeping the Games alive and that she had a good feeling about him.

Frost was initially pleased at his good fortune at finding an apparent ally. However, things took a turn after she set a class project. She correctly sussed out that his partner had not touched the proposal, setting snake muttations upon her and deeply unsettling Frost. Afterwards, Frost expressed his horror of what had happened to his cousin, who urged him to avoid her, but doing so was easier said than done.

When his classmate sneaked into the arena, the Head Gamemaker made Frost go in to rescue him. She was unapologetic about his experience in going to the arena and killing a tribute, and set him a paper, telling him to reflect on the themes of chaos, control and the social contract required for survival. After the Hunger Trials, when Frost enrolled in the Sentinel Corps, the classmate revealed that she had spoken in Frost''s favor, stating that Frost''s actions in cheating for his tribute had only been a piece of faulty strategy. This led Frost to realize that he might still have an ally in her, and so he wrote her a letter. He later sent her a jabberjay with a recording of the classmate describing more planned treasonous activity, which resulted in the classmate''s execution. Ultimately, Frost returned and began attending university thanks to her machinations. Still impressed by his potential, she saw to it that he received an honorable discharge from the Sentinel Corps. She was impressed by what he had learned in the districts and had him study directly under her at the university, additionally granting him a position as an apprentice Gamemaker.

His namesake is a William Shakespeare tragedy, based on a famed Roman general. His surname calls to mind the color white, which he is frequently associated with throughout a trilogy. White typically symbolizes innocence and purity, but it''s also commonly associated with death.

At 18 years old, he was the youngest person to ever pass the Peacekeeper officer candidate test.

He is known to have a granddaughter. This also means he must have had at least one child.

Later in life, Frost seemed to have drifted apart from his cousin due to a combination of her extreme body modification and political pragmatism. However, the fact that she was still alive despite her dissent suggests that he still cared enough to spare her. His cousin appeared to have been disgusted by what Frost eventually became.

He first met a classmate when they were both young. As the classmate and his family were "new money," having originally come from a district, they represented a threat. Despite this, while most of the children took to baiting and belittling him, Frost simply ignored him. In either, the fact that Frost hadn''t belittled him meant that by the time they were both older, the classmate was at least on speaking terms with him. The two both ended up as mentors in the 10th Hunger Trials, and for some reason, Frost found that he kept covering for the classmate when he made disparaging remarks about the Hunger Trials.

Later in life, Frost seemed to have drifted apart from his cousin due to a combination of her extreme body modification and political pragmatism. However, the fact that she was still alive despite her dissent suggests that he still cared enough to spare her. His cousin appeared to have been disgusted by what Frost eventually became.','{"source": "Coriolanus Snow.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 3, "position": 3, "chunk_index": 3, "total_chunks": 4, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Coriolanus Snow.txt", "parent_document_id": "6dde718f-7f32-4ddf-aef0-d37954f9f4d6"}','[0.06057066,0.070763096,0.017976955,0.0051023327,-0.0064478074,-0.085165784,0.0019179612,-0.012703715,-0.023112737,0.039815668,-0.064848624,-0.0735589,0.037535973,-0.017712995,-0.06343275,-0.022786347,0.037632156,0.026681354,0.02866011,0.048966024,-0.017715383,0.01722295,0.010046408,-0.06771621,-0.0051093367,0.027213175,0.015697587,0.016232336,-0.11991686,-0.00016733562,0.019505778,0.058865592,0.0558068,0.039209045,0.018612709,0.066345245,0.013276378,0.0119439885,0.027034981,-0.024314586,0.008019063,0.050340053,0.032245547,0.024229586,0.00016182718,-0.0041943695,0.0043918393,-0.015792804,0.0013622354,0.017046398,0.02091995,-0.0482847,0.021970198,0.0155635625,-0.019553982,-0.015034791,-0.012750351,0.0020581193,-0.010635899,0.02528692,-0.034614895,0.034690857,0.0049336655,0.023020942,0.007794255,-0.035540964,-0.0105300285,-0.0064891786,-0.005217124,0.009545902,0.050133698,0.0012803371,0.015367538,0.0056832326,0.02799529,-0.041878212,-0.071170524,-0.02473325,-0.013090013,0.008556288,0.028050372,0.0027016844,-0.018577123,-0.037705544,0.0035933685,-0.039298613,-0.00073041033,0.007944633,-0.016817251,0.026360676,-0.023064857,0.029226769,-0.006450169,0.029899716,0.02718813,-0.002552821,0.018658428,-0.0030797804,-0.017661348,0.011861076,0.003668213,0.009842671,0.012895407,0.0063376687,0.0034833674,-0.020104123,0.0036237456,-0.024145978,0.010693215,-0.017