	 ('95efbf7d-f04e-42f1-80a8-194b8a390789'::uuid,'The Ballad of Songbirds and Snakes is a prequel book in a franchise. The story follows a top-performing student at the Academy who is selected as part of a new mentor program for the 10th Hunger Trials. His family has fallen upon hard times. A strong performance in the mentor program will likely result in a prize that would allow him to attend the University. He is assigned to mentor the female tribute from District 7, traditionally one of the worst-performing districts.

During the reaping, his tribute performs a stunning song, charming everyone watching. This makes the mentor the envy of his fellow students. He learns that the Academy''s dean is well aware of his family''s poverty and seems to possess an inexplicable grudge against him. The mentor decides to meet his tribute on the train arriving in the Capitol. This results in him being placed with the tributes at the Central Zoo. His bold move endears him to his tribute, whom he learns was part of a traveling group and got trapped in District 7 following the war. He is told that he is good at games and could one day be a Gamemaker. The dean, however, gives him a demerit for his actions.

The mentor continues to visit his tribute at the zoo. Another mentor, originally from a district but now in the Capitol due to wealth, is troubled about the Hunger Trials. He gives his tribute food. Meanwhile, the mentor comes up with the idea of placing odds on the tributes. He continues to bond with his own tribute, as she is one of the few who are willing to engage during interviews. The idea of letting people send food into the arena as a way of getting them more involved is inspired.

Later, another mentor is killed by her tribute, who is then gunned down, making her the first tribute to die before the Games have begun. The mentor is tapped to be the chief mourner at the funeral. He and another student are called away regarding his fleshed-out proposal for a system of betting and sending food into the arena. The other student is punished for lying. The mentor is deeply troubled by the incident. He later finds his tribute starving and becomes even more troubled about her potential fate.

At the funeral, the tributes are paraded. The mentors and tributes are then taken for a tour of the Capitol Coliseum. While there, the arena is bombed. The mentor is trapped by debris, but his tribute rescues him. Several tributes and mentors are killed. The mentor is hospitalized for his injuries. He is pleased to discover his tribute is okay. She tells him he can repay her by thinking that she could actually win. They begin plotting ideas. An assignment is set to write an essay on everything attractive about war. The mentor preps for his tribute''s televised interview, which goes great. He is less-than-satisfied with his paper about the war.

The mentor becomes troubled by everyone''s assessment that his tribute has no chance of winning. He tells her she''ll win and gives her a token. She gives him a kiss. He also strongly hints that she should use the token to smuggle rat poison.

The day of the Hunger Trials dawns with the news that another tribute has died, meaning ten tributes are out before the Games have started. The mentor tries to play up the idea that his tribute is more of a Capitol girl. The mentors are given devices to send in gifts to their tributes based on sponsorship money. The Games begin. An escaped tribute has been chained up, bloodied and beaten. Seeing this, the other mentor smashes a chair and runs out. The gong sounds to start the Games. Later, a tribute kills the chained tribute with an ax, getting the first kill and receiving the first gift.

This is the only action for a long time. It is reminded that the landscape of the Coliseum has changed with the bombings, providing new places to hide.','{"source": "The Ballad of Songbirds and Snakes_Summary.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 0, "position": 0, "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 6, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/The Ballad of Songbirds and Snakes_Summary.txt", "parent_document_id": "24c698c4-dc4b-41aa-9335-49cc2f4a0b42"}','[0.056897383,0.047708374,0.029029667,-0.018038537,0.013699129,-0.04139617,0.021426335,0.009604293,-0.007669538,0.030863503,-0.081916824,-0.07730065,0.044820063,-0.032225598,-0.033547238,-0.065718904,-0.0054250066,0.05812767,0.0007714171,0.05669944,0.009637882,0.04955902,-0.010844468,-0.083110504,0.029518686,-0.00012215362,0.060391888,0.024796395,-0.10972098,-0.00545655,-0.0037607695,0.019462908,-0.024714826,0.07489616,-0.014789172,0.025948688,0.043109998,0.021743216,0.02808758,-0.023258148,-0.0099635385,0.03898344,-0.010696005,0.0041565117,0.008593267,-0.0073008817,0.0023569644,-0.0069210306,-0.014168097,-0.030304106,0.0011587891,-0.03651945,-0.02911751,0.006462918,0.015242082,0.0041846004,-0.0009931168,0.028063813,-0.020004965,0.04782209,-0.0060522305,0.010482805,0.011667451,0.046009265,-0.020760735,-0.020232258,0.011198263,-0.006924017,-0.027730636,0.0047381246,0.024400594,0.01394747,0.013671345,-0.0013638801,0.023445513,-0.016993029,-0.03932317,-0.046241418,-0.028979575,0.018907497,0.036198337,-0.012874559,-0.04854411,-0.027124885,-0.004610263,-0.033159744,-0.030385155,0.0036571233,-0.014425794,0.0098597715,-0.017728714,-0.00095859554,-0.024331296,0.05080076,-0.015230704,-0.028106801,0.013678032,0.035781503,-0.019662485,0.02824055,-0.0068228436,0.012976422,-0.029450675,-0.0070011243,0.0061075627,-0.007755089,-0.0038656692,-0.03173857,-0.004182896,-0.020479454,-0.022790365,-0.010051628,0.016582804,0.009193739,-0.008408132,-0.015172942,0.02184132,0.021164684,0.02759597,0.