	 ('782e201f-2ba4-43cf-86e9-1eaccf5dc3ec'::uuid,'Back at the base, he writes a letter to the high official, reflecting on the balance between chaos and control. That evening, the other mentor helps him study for the officer test, though he doesn''t plan to take it. He suggests that if he were an officer, they might let him train to be a doctor. The other mentor agrees to think it over. The next day, he learns his suggestion of shooting the birds has been approved, but first some must be trapped for study. A researcher comes. The group gets to work and he takes the officer test. Meanwhile, he reflects the other mentor will likely never really change. Getting a suspicion, he examines the other mentor''s effects and discovers a large secret stash of money. Then, he overhears him lying about having no money.

The project to round up the birds continues. He finds the mockingjays unnatural. Despite efforts, they capture jabberjays but no mockingjays. He tells his tribute about the project and she compares the control of the birds to torture. He replies he''s not sure if there''s a human equivalent and she asks if he always feels free to speak his mind. He says you should always say what you think and she tells him her father thought the same and ended up dead. Others then perform a song revealing the origins of her name. He finds the song ridiculous and tries to consult the other mentor, only to find him gone. He quickly locates him but begins to worry he must no longer let him out of his sight. The next day, he talks with his tribute about the Hunger Trials. She asks if he thinks they''re right and he admits he''s not even sure why they do them, but says he thinks people are forgetting the war too fast. She replies her family got broken up because of the Capitol. She feels her freedom is worth the risk and tells him she thinks he wants to think for himself.

She is given a plant, though it''s not ready for eating yet. He learns each member of her group gets their name from a ballad and a color, hers being special because she got her whole name from her ballad. She then performs a soothing song until the melody is picked up by mockingjays. He learns the former lover is trying to convince her to go north to find free people. Though she tells him she trusts him, he is disturbed. The project continues and they finally capture mockingjays. Then, the other mentor pulls him aside. Suspecting a confession, he surreptitiously sets a jabberjay to record. The other mentor reveals he is planning to help rebels leave for the north. They need him to help free the captured woman. He plans to break her out and is buying illegal guns. He tries to talk him out of it, but he''s determined. So he wishes him good luck, but secretly sends the jabberjay with the treasonous words to the high official.

Afterwards, he is guilt-ridden but comforts himself that the recording may never be heard. He worries what his tribute might think. He resolves to try to talk sense into the other mentor but finds no opportunity, and receives a letter of his family''s darkening financial situation. That evening, after a mournful ballad, he discovers the other mentor meeting with the former lover. A shotgun is thrust towards him when his tribute appears and defuses the situation, claiming she''s going north with the former lover. Another man asks if she and the Peacekeeper aren''t together. She replies he is seeing her cousin, and he decides to play along. Then, the mayor''s daughter walks in. The former lover tells her to go home. The other man, however, suggests she join them, saying they have no quarrel with her, only her father.

The mayor''s daughter makes to flee and the other man aims to shoot her, but the former lover knocks the weapon down.','{"source": "The Ballad of Songbirds and Snakes_Summary.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 3, "position": 3, "chunk_index": 3, "total_chunks": 6, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/The Ballad of Songbirds and Snakes_Summary.txt", "parent_document_id": "24c698c4-dc4b-41aa-9335-49cc2f4a0b42"}','[0.033353224,0.034267023,0.014387822,0.008672685,0.0074419137,-0.06356731,-0.000592724,-0.021927174,-0.031233022,0.007816834,-0.08043818,-0.060318768,0.061446205,-0.0310678,-0.035939943,-0.013595849,0.009663563,0.03304565,0.013159874,0.059316773,-0.013545311,0.040985838,0.0020641298,-0.06447787,-0.009596749,-0.006696599,0.069287285,-0.018043801,-0.097264156,0.01029757,-0.018956045,0.028506069,0.0065428023,0.060612097,0.017157612,0.02363091,0.052901503,0.0041304813,0.028095927,-0.029956201,-0.017769594,0.030375686,-0.020687066,0.004190819,0.007307986,-0.0065387124,0.027343852,0.006900018,-0.03810042,-0.07325416,0.024738254,-0.05285251,-0.0033447905,0.0074932324,0.024853503,0.021132842,-0.003146222,0.05252503,-0.006796524,0.02654984,-0.003359095,-0.0151986,0.0029762108,0.01882927,-0.0010208931,-0.029640863,0.0153062325,-0.0048940894,0.010258082,-0.029465567,0.013276225,0.02696793,0.0031886532,0.0050975406,0.0108426465,-0.037210353,-0.06256541,-0.024863008,-0.0049551562,0.026340067,0.015271002,-0.0027995754,-0.026632907,-0.025787508,0.012420374,-0.013617943,-0.030871952,0.01705788,0.010303621,-0.017514767,-0.021894306,-0.012904916,0.04296627,0.03128308,0.022094123,0.0074249157,0.010572086,0.022944508,-0.029246487,0.029075442,0.008038786,0.050801344,-0.010993931,-0.02212268,0.0015828848,-0.020893134,-0.010194928,-0.025048431,-0.0062979143,-0.0020261896,-0.01606006,0.0042314557,0.012341122,0.008183809,0.007707398,-0.023216046,0.015664784,0.02987264,-0.012014993,0.048423152,0.025910804,-0.0033282472,-0.04299746,