	 ('9183c815-3ece-4c8c-8e76-b03028d14713'::uuid,'An arena is a large, enclosed, outdoor area where the Hunger Trials were held each year. Arenas were designed by the Game Masters and a new one was built every year. It could be anything from dense forest to an open meadow, or a frozen wasteland to a scorched desert. The Game Masters planted traps and introduced cunning twists to entertain the people.

The arenas were considered important historical landmarks and were preserved after the conclusion of each year''s competition. They were also popular tourist destinations, where visitors would be able to rewatch the Games, visit the sites of tributes'' deaths, and even take part in reenactments. After the end of the Second Rebellion, all the arenas were destroyed, and memorials were constructed in their place.

Most arenas were seemingly natural environments that were actually under strict control by the Game Masters. They could remotely trigger traps to herd tributes towards one another, unleash vicious Hybrid Mutants upon them, and manipulate the weather conditions, such as temperature and precipitation. They could even artificially simulate different times of day.
Arenas were enclosed by dome-shaped force fields. SkyGlider were able to come and go through them to extract tributes'' remains, and a cloaked hovercraft carrying a huge screen displayed the nightly tally of the dead during the anthem. In some depictions, the dead tributes'' images were instead projected against a false sky. Some arenas had a sun in alignment with the real sun, some had multiple suns, and some had no suns at all.

Underneath each arena was a series of catacombs that held the tributes'' Launch Rooms. Hybrid Mutants also entered and exited the arena via the catacombs. Inside their individual Launch Rooms, tributes changed into their arena wear and stepped onto metal platforms that lifted them into the arena.

The catacombs were a huge subterranean floor underneath the arenas where manual tasks like releasing muttations and stocking up for a feast were carried out. Its existence was kept secret to keep up the pretence that the games were controlled entirely from afar. The most important task here was managing the onsite computer system which was integral when running the Games, kept in a restricted area with high-tech security. It also created and maintained the tribute ecosystem; providing the electricity, plumbing, heating, cooling and ventilation among other things.

Once in the arena, tributes would find themselves arranged in a circle around the Golden Horn. This provided tributes with weapons, food, survival gear, and other useful supplies, if they went in and fought for it at the bloodbath. However, tributes had to remain in place for a sixty-second countdown before the Games could begin, or else they would be blown up by landmines around their platforms.

For a period, the Hunger Trials were hosted in the same location: a dilapidated amphitheater that had been used for sporting, entertainment, and military events prior to the Dark Days, but fell into disrepair afterwards. Tributes were simply thrown into the arena and left to fight. Since there was no cover, the Hunger Trials tended to proceed quickly. Things changed when a bombing of the arena opened up access to various tunnels, resulting in a number of new strategies. Brightly colored snake mutts were released into the arena on the fourth day.

After that, the original arena was abandoned and the Game Masters instead used existing settings in the wild or bombed-out towns and such.

The First Quarter Quell was the first time that the Game Masters built an actual arena for the Hunger Trials.

The arena for the Second Quarter Quell was a big, beautiful meadow, with flowers, streams, pools, birds and a picturesque mountain and sharp, jagged rocks. But everything was lethal.','{"source": "Arena.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 0, "position": 0, "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 3, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Arena.txt", "parent_document_id": "69da43b1-c7fa-4b8e-8792-55aba7bda2c7"}','[0.047005393,0.08553089,-0.03456981,0.013193011,-0.0334947,-0.030963974,-0.018903602,-0.030068902,0.0017851897,0.05052877,-0.021579973,-0.050519586,0.030631881,-0.0029967462,0.008795442,-0.055525735,0.0060915058,0.07953165,-0.047093287,0.10900673,0.03733361,0.038016822,-0.024041448,-0.01580003,0.0062072845,0.007004233,0.054535538,-0.030538779,-0.0838671,-0.039395705,-0.01863728,0.016472997,0.01416171,0.0441997,0.0050199693,0.0049815956,-0.014549845,0.018429298,0.0075597772,0.00617045,0.0019207874,0.029691488,0.032615233,0.0155507205,-0.0008032084,0.04131177,0.025054391,-0.017801536,-0.009569389,-0.007257101,0.019642508,-0.04975747,-0.0016031995,0.035252992,0.051105082,0.0013845204,-0.028402317,-0.0024078093,-0.03604019,0.0039109034,-0.009225224,-0.013657906,-0.016898466,0.043776743,0.002881391,-0.0037866828,-0.0029000465,-0.024780698,-0.011721708,0.00096232165,0.043877497,-0.016826864,0.01675269,-0.01509807,0.011790636,-0.019143758,-0.05562678,-0.053255904,0.012872567,0.013158983,0.019706864,-0.04352209,-0.028840898,-0.024308342,0.007868307,0.002639701,0.00064836803,0.0031601363,-0.005699431,-0.034577623,-0.02153851,0.04004069,-0.012679907,0.048188273,-0.00281615,-0.021891668,0.033742066,0.029951626,-0.036511928,0.030848172,0.0037065202,0.0056738006,-0.040082604,0.006644951,-0.0061781313,0.0010488986,-0.0073260274,-0.019201456,-0.002914669,-0.020120561,0.0054237195,-0.0020455818,0.012285429,0.024210414,-0.010922146,0.024412595,0.019204432,0.024581376,0.02718709,0.042551156,0.027934339,0.020195307,0.014902672,0.0449