	 ('c930118b-b8ec-40df-8133-9b8dcc6c4b3d'::uuid,'The tributes of these Games had to face stinging butterflies; fluffy, golden squirrels which were in fact carnivorous; the picturesque mountain that was actually a volcano; flowers that could poison someone if inhaled too directly; pink birds with skewer‑like beaks; and dehydration, due to the fact that the only sources of water that weren''t toxic were from the bounty at the Golden Horn or rainfall.

The arena for the 74th Hunger Trials was a large expanse of various terrains. In the center around the Golden Horn where the tributes launched was a plain of hard packed dirt. In one direction, there was a wheat field. In another direction there was a large lake. In the other two directions, there was a forest which made up most of the arena. In the woods, there was a stream that led to the lake, and a rocky area with numerous caves beside it. There were also several Game Master Hybrid Mutants: locator jackers, mockingjays, and wolf mutts. The forest was rigged with traps such as devices that could launch fireballs. The Game Masters could manipulate the temperature so that it was freezing at night and hot during the day, even drying up the stream later into the Games.

In a film depiction, the arena was mostly a wooded forest, with mountainous higher areas and flat plains. A single large river ran through the east of the arena that fed into the large lake by the Golden Horn.

The arena for the Third Quarter Quell was set up like a clock. The 12 to 1 wedge consisted of a tall tree that was periodically struck by lightning during the 12th hour, which proved to be a very important factor later on. The other sections of the "clock" each had a tall tree identical to the 12 to 1 tree to throw tributes off, and were made up of different "horrors" that were unleashed by the hour. Known threats included blood rain, acid fog, vicious monkey mutts, jabberjays, an unknown beast, a tidal wave, and unknown insects.

This arena was very small, and circular in shape, enclosed by a powerful force field. The Golden Horn lay at its center, tail pointing in the direction of the 12th hour, and was surrounded by water and twelve spokes between each set of two tributes as they were first raised into the arena. The trees were the only source of drinkable water, which had to be extracted by a spile or by hacking at the bark. In a film depiction, this arena appeared to be made of a series of hexagonal screens mounted to a girder system with a force field on the inside that prevented damage from occurring.

On the third night inside the arena, a tribute blew out the force field during the midnight lightning storm, allowing rebels to perform an extraction by hovercraft.

For the 49th Hunger Trials the arena was full of shiny surfaces lakes that reflected the sky clouds that turned to vapor and boulders and caves and cliffs overlayed with mirrors. The Game Masters called it the Nest of Mirrors.

The 70th Hunger Trials arena had a dam that was later broken by an earthquake, flooding the arena.

Some other arenas are mentioned, but it is unknown in which Hunger Trials they appeared.

A few years before the 50th Hunger Trials, there was an arena that would go darkish without any warning. Out of the shadows, giant coal black weasels with pointy teeth would appear and attack the tributes.

A ruined city arena is briefly shown in a film depiction.

There once was a freezing cold arena with no firewood. The audience considered this "anticlimactic" because so many tributes huddled into balls and quietly froze to death.

One arena had a landscape of boulders, sand, and scruffy bushes. Many tributes were bitten by venomous snakes or went insane from thirst.','{"source": "Arena.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 1, "position": 1, "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 3, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Arena.txt", "parent_document_id": "69da43b1-c7fa-4b8e-8792-55aba7bda2c7"}','[0.012533709,0.06082283,-0.01704356,0.041290678,-0.012297471,-0.038900882,-0.008002804,-0.043739162,2.9620014e-06,0.05328672,-0.026641443,-0.058387533,0.058594048,-0.054222405,0.0049441583,-0.026962353,-0.0017333116,0.046956215,0.0049366006,0.09980628,0.026805744,-0.0025561657,-0.018631864,-0.046676725,0.0020950253,0.005107266,-0.0057346663,-0.029266156,-0.118656,-0.05254651,-0.0016211298,-0.01098384,-0.015223441,0.025212197,-0.03586263,-0.019793475,-0.012008284,-0.0011157305,0.0006009836,-0.04010432,0.008157833,0.015604921,0.0409247,0.0066164304,-0.0075475336,-8.344472e-05,0.020715786,0.0067443354,-0.0052199108,-0.022599557,0.03697436,-0.04779711,-0.008235916,0.049487785,0.04600856,-0.0025018258,-0.018288754,0.012271063,-0.041994806,0.0052583218,-0.034873437,-0.0039196154,0.0044975034,0.028996963,0.010048134,0.0035763718,0.0136318505,-0.041961864,0.0066121398,-0.00038962308,0.014868677,-0.0066628833,0.044564724,-0.03419714,0.014566134,-0.023841796,-0.04923476,-0.069993705,0.0048805214,0.013226738,0.0147040915,-0.03989706,-0.04513141,-0.03611907,0.03232052,-0.013012983,-0.019680655,-0.003171669,0.0051447963,-0.0009696185,-0.009383367,0.033318516,-0.0066094478,0.052445598,0.0060859206,-0.017002048,0.03175053,0.0138342995,-0.039146774,0.019188127,-0.008408577,0.027108056,-0.0500827,-0.0013326303,-0.010999566,-0.023505758,0.0062489584,-0.017387489,0.013845765,-0.0066178427,-0.02032071,0.003225117,0.02547841,0.027494181,0.009893803,0.007961764,0.008175712,0.003580216,0.013139025,0.048066307,0.06693333,0.015558916,-0.011589931,0.06346354,0.037415706,0.017585505,-0