	 ('60b06b92-b6b8-40b6-aaf8-fde86efc8a33'::uuid,'During the first nine The Fire Games, tributes were held in the Sentinel Corps'' stables. Soon after, they were locked inside the Capitol Coliseum with nothing but weapons while the audience watched from the comfort of their own homes. At the conclusion of each competition, the bodies would be removed and the doors locked until the next year with no further fanfare. Certain districts produced more victors in these years, while others were also considered contenders. There was no history of muttations ever being used in the first nine years of the Games.

On the first evening of the The Hunger Games: Ten, it was said that in terms of fatalities, they were running neck and neck with the previous year. This implies that during the 9th Hunger Contest, around ten tributes had died by the afternoon on the first day.

The Hunger Games: Ten was the first to introduce mentors. It also introduced betting, sponsorship, sponsor gifts, and interviews. Additionally, Mutt Serpents were released into the arena, making them the first mutts to be used in this way. Prior to the Games, tributes were held in a zoo, and they later competed inside the Capitol Coliseum, the landscape of which had been altered by a bombing.

Many tributes and their mentors were killed before the Games officially began, and the victor won by cheating with the help of her mentor. Written off as an embarrassing failure, all but one recording of the The Hunger Games: Ten were destroyed, though aspects like the interviews, betting, sponsorship, and mentorship would be carried forward in future editions.

After the failure of the previous year, the Gamemakers brainstormed ways to engage the districts and the Capitol for the 12th Hunger Games. New additions included incentives to send forward stronger tributes, such as food parcels for the winning district, the creation of the Champion''s Village, and monetary prizes for the victors themselves. The victor this year became the first to embark on a Triumphal Journey.

This was the first 25-Year Hunger Games. Every twenty-five years, a new twist would be introduced for a special edition of the The Fire Games. The twist this year was that each district had to elect its own tributes. The victor of these Games died sometime before the third 25-Year Hunger Games, which was held fifty years later.

One victor won this year by setting up an electrical trap with parts scavenged from his arena. It was said he had electrocuted six tributes at once.

These Games were not mentioned in the original trilogy. According to one source, a victor sustained a broken neck in the final showdown.

One victor lost his hand during these Games. He did not accept a prosthesis.

A victor from a specific district was the victor of these Games.

The arena for the 49th The Fire Games was full of shiny surfaces and lakes. The boulders, caves and cliffs were overlayed with mirrors. The Gamemakers introduced shiny silver beetles that swarmed and suffocated the tributes. The victor was from a specific district.

For the second 25-Year Hunger Games, two boys and two girls were reaped from each district, bringing the total number of tributes to 48. These Games were won by a victor who survived an arena full of poison, deadly mutts, and an active volcano. His last opponent was killed when she unknowingly threw her axe into a force field, which redirected the weapon back at her.

During her Games, a victor killed one tribute by ripping open his throat with her teeth. This made her very popular, and after her victory, she surgically altered her teeth so each one ended in a point like a fang, inlaid with gold.

A victor from a specific district was the victor of these Games.','{"source": "List of Hunger Games.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 0, "position": 0, "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 3, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/List of Hunger Games.txt", "parent_document_id": "7e9ac567-7b9e-4ff2-adbe-deaf499efe7c"}','[0.028219352,0.08309705,-0.028908791,-0.0041275574,0.0040032305,-0.022223378,-0.007777171,-0.005679242,-0.027809104,0.01768622,-0.028946232,-0.024401573,0.057884097,0.020543486,-0.037719294,-0.065882124,0.013110005,0.05043846,-0.043644175,0.03420907,0.012997374,0.037449893,0.0031936532,-0.046752464,0.0076712184,-0.0056167794,0.04534631,-0.0065319585,-0.12088556,-0.029688908,-0.0070196614,-0.0033910603,0.009105027,0.03971406,-0.018384952,-0.005144716,0.008516069,0.010464714,-0.001588314,-0.013330496,-0.010015228,-0.017162357,0.02030223,0.0046418863,0.023137974,0.033508457,0.051553834,-0.006126545,0.005219499,0.007113177,0.02457204,-0.035414655,-0.042029995,0.013033208,0.021773824,0.009915844,0.0036363362,0.020298067,-0.049416326,-0.011925394,-0.040228087,0.009128088,0.0035359298,0.05513185,-0.00975312,-0.028627163,-0.028843468,-0.034263927,-0.013188399,0.0006413703,0.05742488,-0.012312222,0.04002865,0.013536685,0.018211288,-0.042627834,-0.05637817,-0.04942423,-0.0020147872,0.023802176,0.016666306,-0.04003824,-0.04322625,-0.029716872,0.024534153,-0.033629086,-0.016717141,-0.0011646219,0.0031484305,0.012997049,-0.014132802,0.035023846,-0.038421404,0.05250895,0.0043906625,-0.006404232,0.00903735,0.02156102,-0.027207002,0.011445686,-0.031108133,0.021060014,-0.030868703,-0.0042169904,0.006486721,-0.016822571,-0.0041349255,-0.032017495,0.029696828,-0.0120513495,-0.014560048,-0.02198455,0.017854974,0.042766865,-0.01338293,0.0144782625,0.006907116,-0.0025752808,0.033457804,0.04817922,0.006205348,0.002545547,-0.034593314,0.036610115,0.006401361,-0.0028270467,-0.019451791,-0.03592304,0.044842534,0.02915021,-0.00