	 ('3f44d57a-5ade-42e7-b997-b98d6da0674c'::uuid,'Being from a Career district, it''s assumed that this victor was in an alliance with the other Career tributes. The victor''s sibling won the following year.

The victor this year was from a specific district. She''s presumed to have been in a Career alliance, and her sibling won the year prior.

A victor was the youngest victor in The Fire Games history at the age of 14. As a Career tribute, he was adept with spears and knives, and he held his own in the arena until his many sponsors, drawn to him by his good looks, gifted him a particularly expensive trident. At this point, he secured his victory by netting other tributes and stabbing them with his trident.

These Games were not mentioned in the original trilogy. The victor was known by a specific nickname and hailed with a specific title.

While these games were not mentioned in the original trilogy, the arena was a "burning desert". One character recalled an edition of the The Fire Games where the arena was made up of sand, boulders, and scruffy bushes, and many tributes went insane from thirst. This was likely these Games.

A victor was traumatized after seeing a fellow tribute die. She hid for the remainder of the Games until an earthquake broke a dam, flooding the arena. Since she came from the fishing district, she was the best swimmer, so she was able to outlast the remaining tributes.

One victor pretended to be weak and fragile so others would underestimate her before she revealed her dangerous competence in the arena.

According to an interview, this was a specific Head Gamemaker''s first year in that role.

The victor of these Games was an unnamed male from a specific district, assumed to be a member of a Career alliance. During the final battle, he used a brick to kill another tribute. This was the Head Gamemaker''s second year in that role.

This was the Head Gamemaker''s third and final year in that role. When two tributes threatened double suicide by deadly nightlock berries, leaving the Capitol at risk of having no victor at all, both were allowed to win. This would be the inciting incident for a series of uprisings, leading to the eventual revolution.

For the third 25-Year Hunger Games, the tributes were reaped from their district''s existing pool of victors. This was a new Head Gamemaker''s first and only year in that role. The Games were interrupted by a rebel plan to break out of the arena, so no victor was ever crowned, though six tributes survived. This was the final Games ever held, and the Games were abolished after the The Great Uprising.

The idea of a Annual Hunger Games was put forth by a political leader. She proposed that tributes be reaped from the children of Capitol officials, in order to satisfy calls for bloodshed. This proposal was voted upon by the victors who survived the war, but while it was approved, it was never officially held.

In one account, two victors from the same district won their respective Games with the use of camouflage. Another account mentions a male victor who was deeply mentally affected following his games and needed to be comforted by his mentor. He won about ten years prior to the 2nd 25-Year Hunger Games. No specific details have been given about the The Fire Games won by several other victors.

One year, the tributes had to bludgeon each other to death with spiked maces, the only available weapons.

One arena had a landscape of boulders, sand, and scruffy bushes. Many tributes were bitten by venomous snakes or went insane from thirst.

Another year, half of the tributes froze to death at night because they had no firewood. The Capitol audience considered this anticlimactic because so many tributes huddled into balls and quietly frozen to death.','{"source": "List of Hunger Games.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 1, "position": 1, "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 3, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/List of Hunger Games.txt", "parent_document_id": "7e9ac567-7b9e-4ff2-adbe-deaf499efe7c"}','[0.034857005,0.08084755,-0.01454191,-0.0054971925,-0.009661411,-0.052462004,0.016718496,0.0030750162,-0.021893036,0.023883779,-0.051689282,-0.090623975,0.023370842,-0.00079262786,-0.024423284,-0.06002713,0.018865818,0.04206342,-0.021063667,0.034334164,0.008204222,0.029930048,-0.003955301,-0.05435143,0.017253486,-0.0068656304,0.031178119,-0.0070048855,-0.12792143,-0.0359965,0.013349251,-0.009319633,0.034930017,0.044656985,0.008957908,0.022106722,-0.018664017,-0.0014878972,-0.010254118,-0.006217764,-0.004952924,0.007330556,0.044863645,-0.004327873,0.039105218,0.018110583,0.021204276,-0.013856197,0.030592052,0.025390033,0.022276655,-0.02847455,-0.0017352839,0.04541733,0.044519346,0.014340365,0.007792835,0.0034148714,-0.031739544,0.017890437,-0.03338279,0.02535028,-0.0011568642,0.024045574,0.008263729,-0.004134115,-0.043574963,0.004872022,-0.026600929,0.018585293,0.0368447,-0.020677049,0.047852006,-0.008988573,0.015381937,-0.04450341,-0.0499182,-0.03905783,-0.01218778,0.0130141135,0.020671707,-0.019572865,-0.046464078,-0.02661414,0.048539434,-0.028828993,-0.022602316,0.0021996328,0.0046875086,0.0032829125,-0.021712609,0.029414965,-0.043434653,0.026387999,-0.008719741,-0.017874358,0.0038863493,-0.0054575135,-0.035411935,0.031034423,-0.027828008,0.040540393,0.0024560387,0.016697789,0.011368336,-0.010963447,0.018556874,-0.027098948,0.02635564,-0.033122852,-0.026892656,-0.017340448,-0.0043193577,0.018213332,-0.019238941,0.0049653477,-0.0144978585,-0.009045044,0.03