95,-0.035580188,0.0016579916,0.02258471,-0.013579175,0.029274922,0.021464994,-0.0035376651,-0.009522249,-0.027930617,-0.04515483,0.00069149566,-0.017876726,0.044314817,-0.06837635,0.015578852,0.008178157,0.030409502,-0.015675094,-0.010842327,-0.01918279,0.011811083,0.022897512,0.0009599848,0.016322909,0.06397197,0.012755416,0.024676993,-0.018729137,-0.010967907,-0.008227501,-0.044902336,0.014632907,0.014699037,0.013808207,0.05642042,0.05337175,0.0053174747,-0.023658236,0.015890379,-0.010431739,-0.017393628,0.019145368,-0.033615664,-0.0120745525,-0.031067612,0.0039461744,0.0021990882,-0.008496699,0.031330165,0.03413688,0.052139785,0.015935924,0.0030855322,0.0025725048,-0.01756851,0.012241201,0.027740192,0.008743962,0.0023897043,0.0019368959,-0.020533307,0.011753602,-0.0045526703,-0.0027272964,-0.0060012015,-0.0012093825,0.0021776229,-0.028992964,0.012056209,-0.013509464,-0.037808128,0.018507715,0.008837959,-0.019379573,0.025256582,0.0066512725,-0.006235487,-0.0147119565,0.0053729075,-0.010064455,-0.03751537,-0.032720402,0.0068590348,0.010894889,0.040033422,-0.0043213195,-0.005328556,-0.016850337,-0.050499,0.01872377,-0.005787344,-0.025942141,-0.017830476,-0.030119056,0.006527928,0.0049317204,-0.0066381786,0.019317795,0.007958526,0.018362906,-0.015886951,0.022584531,0.009160956,0.0059268167,-0.02566118,0.0042543686,-0.0099068545,-0.025605496,0.011961295,0.028716773,0.030276835,-0.01114945,-0.018308042,-0.016035173,-0.041006908,-0.004255111,0.037307624,0.03646729,-0.019948987,0.058095932,0.019447144,0.04703246,-0.022939354,-0.011455062,0.023259452,0.027569445,0.026593424,0.012342377]'::public.vector),
	 ('841b586e-a0f8-4517-8fcc-3a446f424232'::uuid,'Coalitions were an important and recurring feature of the Hunger Trials, formed when tributes recognized the benefits of cooperation to improve their chances of survival. By pooling resources such as food, water, supplies, and weapons, allies could protect one another from immediate threats in the arena and increase their collective odds of outlasting other tributes.

Despite these advantages, alliances of any form were inherently fragile. With the Hunger Trials mandating that only one tribute could become a victor, alliances, in their simplest form, were temporary arrangements of convenience rather than lasting bonds. As the Games advanced, alliances typically broke down - sometimes violently, other times through mutual parting to delay the emotional burden of betrayal. Regardless of form, the prospect of eventual confrontation always hung over allied tributes until the Games'' conclusion.

Common types
District Partners
Coalitions between tributes from the same district were among the most common and natural partnerships in the Hunger Trials. Such alliances often formed early, sometimes immediately after the reaping or in the lead up to the training period. District partners frequently shared a pre-existing bond which could foster trust and cooperation. However, this was not always the case; in many cases, tributes from the same district may have had little prior connection at all.
District Ally alliances offered several natural advantages over ad hoc arrangements formed in the arena. District partners had more time to coordinate strategies, assess one another''s strengths and weaknesses, and benefit jointly from the advice of their mentor(s). These advantages gave them a stronger foundation of cooperation once the Games began.

Career Coalitions
The Career tributes - volunteer tributes from specific districts - were distinguished by their lifelong preparation for the Hunger Trials. From a young age, many attended specialized academies in their districts, where they trained in combat, survival, and weapons use. This upbringing gave them a significant advantage and instilled a sense of entitlement and confidence in their prospects of victory.
For the Games, Careers typically formed the most dominant and feared alliance, colloquially known as the "Career Pack" or simply "the Pack." True to their name, this alliance employed aggressive, pack-like tactics: monopolizing the Cornucopia, securing resources, and systematically hunting down weaker tributes. The Career alliance usually consisted of multiple members from specific districts, though its size could vary if they invited non-Career tributes or excluded weaker volunteers.
The Career alliance''s unity and strength made them near unstoppable in the early stages of the Games, and it was common for one of their members to eventually emerge as victor. For other tributes, the Career Pack represented one of the main threats they had to contend with, both strategically and psychologically.

Inter-district Coalitions
Inter-district alliances were less common than district partnerships or the Career pack, but occasionally formed when tributes from different districts recognized the strategic value of cooperation. These alliances typically arose out of necessity or convenience, usually when the Games were already underway, allowing tributes from diverse backgrounds to pool skills, resources, and knowledge in order to survive environmental hazards in the arena or resist stronger opponents, particularly the Careers.
Unlike district partnerships or the Career pack, inter-district alliances lacked the foundation of shared upbringing or long-term training and often were formed haphazardly. As a result, they were generally more fragile and prone to early collapse, as mutual distrust and the absence of deep personal ties left them vulnerable to betrayal or abandonment.
A notable exception was a large alliance formed in one specific Games. This unprecedented coalition included many tributes from non-Career districts, making it the largest alliance in Hunger Trials history. Although many of its members died in the opening bloodbath, the survivors maintained their unity throughout the Games and ultimately secured victory for the alliance. This level of cohesion was unique amongst the non-Career districts.

Role in the Games
Purpose and benefits
