	 ('b9ad0eed-19b3-46e8-b622-7a10dd7827f8'::uuid,'By pooling skills, resources, and knowledge, tributes could compensate for individual weaknesses and improve both their short- and long-term prospects. Weaker tributes gained protection from stronger allies, while combat-trained tributes benefited from tributes with specialized skills such as first aid, scouting, foraging, or tactical planning. Alliances also provided emotional support, helping tributes cope with the isolation and paranoia they faced while being in the unfamiliar arena.
A second major benefit was the attraction of sponsors. Tributes who demonstrated teamwork, strategy, and survival skills were more compelling to audiences, making alliances an effective way to draw sponsor interest. Gifts sent to alliances tended to be larger or more valuable, as they could sustain multiple tributes and extend the drama of the Games. Career alliances particularly benefited greatly from this system, as their members'' reputation and odds of winning made them reliable investments for sponsors.
Finally, alliances allowed for the strategic distribution of resources. Sponsor gifts and supplies taken from the Cornucopia could be rationed and allocated according to each member''s strengths or needs, ensuring a group''s overall effectiveness. This cooperation, however temporary, often gave alliances an early and lasting advantage over isolated tributes.

Weaknesses and dangers
Despite their advantages, alliances were inherently fragile and temporary. Trust was always precarious, as tributes had to balance cooperation with the knowledge that their allies would eventually become rivals. Betrayal could occur at any moment when the partnership ceased to be beneficial or even become a liability.
Alliances could also create uneven burdens. Partnerships involving weaker or dependent tributes sometimes endangered the group as a whole. In some cases, a tribute might sacrifice themselves to ensure the younger or stronger members could escape a threat, recognizing that their presence was slowing the group down.
Finally, alliances were often more conspicuous targets. Groups of tributes were easier to detect, and rival alliances or solo tributes could exploit their visibility. In that same vein, shared resources, while a major strength, could also become a liability if stolen, destroyed, or mismanaged, creating tension and undermining group cohesion.

Dissolution
No alliance in the Hunger Trials could last indefinitely. The principle of the Hunger Trials as a battle royale - that only one tribute could survive to become the victor - ensured that even the closest partnerships would eventually collapse. The single exception was one specific Games, when a mid-Games rule change briefly allowed for two victors from the same district. When the Gamemakers revoked the rule to force a final confrontation, the pair threatened mutual suicide, forcing the Gamemakers to declare them both victors.
In most Games, alliances dissolved naturally as the field narrowed. Partnerships ended through betrayal, abandonment, elimination by opponents, arena hazards, or Gamemaker manipulation. The nature of dissolution depended on the bond between allies and the circumstances of the arena. Some allies chose to part ways peacefully rather than risk a direct confrontation. By contrast, Career alliances were particularly prone to violent implosion. Once they had eliminated weaker tributes, the aggressive nature of their training and strategy often turned inward, forcing members to kill one another in brutal, final confrontations.
The physical, emotional, and psychological toll of surviving to the endgame virtually guaranteed that every alliance, regardless of its form, would ultimately dissolve to pave the way for the sole victor.','{"source": "Alliance.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 1, "position": 1, "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 2, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Alliance.txt", "parent_document_id": "9bcefef5-c25a-498d-911b-c929398560e4"}','[0.031033395,0.06829114,0.0032253244,0.014124232,0.029693991,0.022403462,0.008022576,0.0026637036,-0.005006689,0.005994276,-0.09310947,-0.04100015,-0.0012052588,-0.0031169986,-0.026232902,0.0038772165,0.0023524687,0.07999122,-0.026101053,0.03918979,0.039644126,0.007885364,0.018108858,-0.045683015,-0.019245496,0.003475634,0.109829344,-0.018709466,-0.104429945,-0.008349107,-0.035536822,0.0063547,-0.02406006,0.041200854,0.009028322,0.01922392,0.017364234,0.0003590406,0.024510583,-0.025135677,0.036585093,-0.015100972,0.0217683,0.02349132,0.00490076,0.016563965,0.003990542,-0.05249108,-0.0027826405,-0.010221979,0.013099021,-0.006956718,-0.012633109,0.029801156,0.082406536,0.01510932,-0.005011267,0.009807925,-0.023911022,-0.0060789916,0.013864091,0.029498506,0.013958283,0.00093838386,0.009802943,-0.026969513,-0.040837295,-0.03725829,-0.006330838,0.04650708,0.066151686,0.007511721,0.0064006355,0.00081684097,-0.002386005,-0.018398918,-0.05109122,-0.04335333,0.0016429061,0.054109722,0.0055123013,-0.016956374,0.0020074388,-0.018039696,0.03598293,-0.022379387,-0.034346227,0.009289008,-0.0030998471,0.00055812224,-0.06889688,0.04300596,0.011551885,0.041871395,0.019496432,0.01061046,0.041815672,0.041633807,-0.046185374,0.00225172,-0.0028347902,0.026491595,0.010482746,-0.01409645,0.035372775,-0.0152856605,-0.010749568,-0.016501293,0.028249433,-0.01739842,-0.020580964,0.00256984,0.029146574,-0.021350343,-0.02038482,-0.019262705,0.024840973,0.0060095754,0.03721361,0.049278453,0.0076685413,0.0009225966,-0