	 ('23deec65-cf9d-45da-ac66-dd062c03227a'::uuid,'The protagonist loads her arrow.
She remembers a conversation with the ruler in which they promised not to lie to each other. She realizes that the ruler was telling the truth about the rebel leader ordering the children to be killed, knowing that by sending her sister into battle she would be killed by the bombings, making the protagonist unstable and the rebel leader the next suitable candidate. When she is supposed to execute the ruler, she kills the rebel leader instead. A riot ensues and the ruler begins laughing, choking up blood. He is found mysteriously dead. The protagonist is acquitted due to her apparent insanity along with testimony to justify her action, which helped reclaim her reputation as a hero. She and her mentor return to their home district. Her mother moves to another district as home has too many sad memories. Two crew members are sent out into the districts to help people affected by the war. A victor gives birth to her deceased lover''s son. The ally returns months after the protagonist and her mentor arrived, having recovered immensely from his brainwashing. Finally, the protagonist understands that falling in love with the ally was inevitable, as he had always represented the promise of a better future. Together with her mentor, they create a book filled with the stories of the previous tributes and others who died in the war so that they will not be forgotten. At the end, the protagonist and the ally play a last game. The ally asks, "You love me? Real or not real?" The protagonist replies without hesitation and whispers, "Real."
In the epilogue, the protagonist speaks as an adult, more than fifteen years later. She and the ally grow back together. They have two children together. The Hunger Games are over, but she dreads the day her children''s innocent world will be shattered by learning the details of their parents'' involvement in both the Games and the war. When she feels upset, she takes to reminding herself of every good thing that she has ever seen someone do.','{"source": "Mockingjay.txt", "charset": "UTF-8", "chunk_id": 4, "position": 4, "chunk_index": 4, "total_chunks": 5, "source_document": "/Users/artem/IdeaProjects/practicum/sprint07/ragbot/./data/replaced/Mockingjay.txt", "parent_document_id": "9579a7da-3de6-4355-83ad-391021ff9f15"}','[0.028787913,0.044894475,0.025631437,-0.026892165,0.008077435,-0.024641324,0.008710025,0.04941783,-0.05069812,-0.009412295,-0.08760554,-0.054743256,0.006877908,-0.03358088,-0.040168338,0.018952953,0.029661292,0.041326784,0.00587422,0.07172732,-0.0023852591,0.024804173,-0.006333605,-0.043911573,0.013587055,-0.00086342945,0.008218114,-0.0040825764,-0.10415391,0.017232282,0.020934567,0.01739536,-0.021285148,0.056229994,0.005883382,0.03537868,0.0088253245,0.005114563,0.0052629113,-0.014431303,0.009721306,0.032057997,0.020504845,0.0028876148,0.009440621,-0.019914778,-0.0041785226,-0.0006077032,0.012100624,-0.026856337,-0.014484119,-0.034943577,0.021360217,-0.0059029134,0.06818894,-0.009503688,0.014661968,0.026122604,-0.031886656,-0.0025213775,0.0131076155,-0.012353992,0.027181305,0.0071376823,0.021853484,-0.0066574337,0.0074567315,-0.006819672,-0.007736404,0.001801966,0.011936906,-0.023455042,-0.022755481,0.016747735,0.025795074,-0.04187619,-0.07203937,-0.031215975,-0.0022417966,0.018791998,0.0085371975,0.0005074274,-0.036074404,-0.009539136,0.012674391,-0.029459648,-0.02126696,0.006176931,0.0003412216,-0.017520811,-0.026306514,0.04168956,-0.00879664,0.026439982,0.01324462,-0.035037294,-0.01789114,0.0022273578,-0.024099287,0.018860798,0.002573994,0.02